WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.106
A word problem becomes manageable when quantities are named before calculating

00:00:05.106 --> 00:00:09.811
A delivery service charges five dollars plus two dollars per kilometer

00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:15.800
Let d measure distance in kilometers and c measure the total charge in dollars.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.340
For a four-kilometer delivery, the variable part is eight dollars, then the fixed fee adds five

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.508
The total thirteen dollars includes both contributions

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:32.033
Units prevent mistaking the five-dollar fee for five additional kilometers.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.025
A nineteen-dollar bill reverses the question

00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:44.550
Remove the fixed five-dollar fee to isolate fourteen dollars of distance charges, then divide by two dollars per kilometer

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.933
The resulting seven has units of kilometers, matching the unknown we named.

00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:54.138
Finally check the model against the story

00:00:54.138 --> 00:01:01.608
Nonnegative distances make the total at least five dollars, so a three-dollar bill cannot fit this pricing rule

00:01:01.608 --> 00:01:07.833
A mathematically computed answer may be invalid if it violates the context or its units.
